As an IT student, technology is a big part of my daily life. I use many apps on my phone and laptop without always thinking about how they work behind the scenes. 
Studying cloud computing helps me understand how these digital tools actually deliver their services over the internet.
Every day, I rely on three main cloud applications: Google Drive, Netflix, and Canva. I use Google Drive to store my school projects and documents. 
I use Netflix to stream movies during my free time. Finally, I use Canva to create presentations and posters for my subjects.
All three of these applications use the Software as a Service (SaaS) model. This means I can access and use the software directly through a web browser or mobile app. 
I do not need to install complex program files, write code, or manage any servers. The companies take care of all the updates and technical maintenance for me. 
In terms of deployment models, all three run on a Public Cloud. The infrastructure is owned by cloud providers, 
and services are delivered to millions of users over the public internet.
Beyond using apps, I am also learning how developers build and maintain cloud systems using version control tools like Git and GitHub. 
Version control is critical when working with cloud infrastructure. Cloud setups often rely on configuration files written as code. 
If a developer makes a mistake in the code, it can cause an entire website or cloud system to crash.
Git and GitHub help teams track every change made to these files over time. 
If something breaks, developers can quickly see who made the change and roll back to a working version. 
GitHub also lets team members collaborate on the same project without overwriting each other’s work. It keeps our projects safe, organized, and error-free. 
